<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Accessibility service configuration for the floating-dictation auto-insert.

  Narrow by design (Google Play "Use of AccessibilityService APIs" and data
  minimisation): the service exists only to put already-recognised text into the
  text field the user is currently focused on.

  Compared with the previous configuration:
    * typeViewClicked and typeViewTextSelectionChanged were removed. The service
      only needs to know which editable node has input focus
      (typeViewFocused) and to drop a remembered node when the window changes
      (typeWindowStateChanged). Selection changes are read from the focused node
      at insert time, not subscribed to.
    * flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows was removed. The service never enumerates
      windows — it reads getRootInActiveWindow() for the single focused field —
      so requesting visibility of every interactive window was more access than
      the feature uses.
    * android:label and android:description were both pointing at the app name;
      they now describe what the service does, because that text is what the
      user reads in Android Settings when granting or revoking access.

  canRetrieveWindowContent stays true: reading the focused field's text and
  selection is the entire purpose. Nothing is persisted between uses — see
  FloatingDictationAccessibilityService, which holds at most one node reference
  and releases it on window change, unbind and destroy.

  android:isAccessibilityTool="false" is declared explicitly, and that is the
  honest value: this is a dictation convenience, not a service whose primary
  purpose is to help people with disabilities use their device, so the app is
  not eligible to claim the accessibility-tool exemption. Platform behaviour is
  unchanged by writing it out — AccessibilityServiceInfo parses the attribute
  with a default of false (frameworks/base, AccessibilityServiceInfo.java:
  `asAttributes.getBoolean(R.styleable.AccessibilityService_isAccessibilityTool,
  false)`) — but stating it removes any ambiguity during Play review, where the
  attribute decides whether the prominent-disclosure requirement applies.
  Because the service is not an accessibility tool, the app ships the separate
  in-context disclosure required by Play's "Use of the AccessibilityService API"
  policy: AccessibilityDisclosureActivity, reachable only by the user asking for
  the feature, with explicit Continue/Not now consent before Android's own
  Accessibility settings are opened.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewFocused|typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="false"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="100" />
